Added `cancel` to `src/main/java/mn/pay/PaymentService.java:36`:

```java
@Transactional
public Payment cancel(String paymentId) {
    Payment p =
            repository
                    .findById(paymentId)
                    .orElseThrow(
                            () -> new IllegalArgumentException("unknown payment: " + paymentId));
    if (!"PENDING".equals(p.getStatus())) {
        throw new IllegalStateException(
                "only a PENDING payment can be cancelled, was: " + p.getStatus());
    }
    p.setStatus("CANCELLED");
    return repository.save(p);
}
```

Decisions I made, since the request didn't pin them down:
- **Unknown id** → `IllegalArgumentException`, matching how `PaymentValidator` reports bad caller input.
- **Non-PENDING status** → `IllegalStateException` rather than a silent no-op, so a double cancel or a cancel of a settled payment is visible to the caller. If you'd rather `cancel` be idempotent (already-CANCELLED returns as-is), that's a one-line change.
- Kept the `"PENDING"`/`"CANCELLED"` string literals to match `create`, rather than introducing a status enum.

`mvn -o compile` and `mvn -o test` both pass.

Two things I deliberately left alone:

- **I did not expose it over GraphQL.** You asked for the service method; `payment.graphqls` and `PaymentController` still only have `createPayment`. Say the word and I'll add the `cancelPayment` mutation and controller method.
- **`@Transactional` alone does not make this concurrency-safe.** Under READ COMMITTED, two concurrent cancels can both read PENDING and both write CANCELLED. The transaction scopes the check and write together but doesn't serialize them — that needs a pessimistic lock (`@Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)` on a repository lookup) or a conditional update. I kept the method matching the existing code's approach rather than changing the locking strategy unasked; worth doing if double-cancel has real consequences downstream.

Separately, and pre-existing: `create` only sets `status` — it never sets `id`, `idempotencyKey`, or `amount`, all of which are `@Column(nullable = false)` (and `id` has no generation strategy). That looks like it would fail on insert against a real database. Outside the scope of this change, but you'll hit it before `cancel` has anything to cancel.
